---
import DownloadButton from '../../components/DownloadButton.astro'
import StarButton from '../../components/StarButton.astro'
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro'
import { latestVersion } from '../../lib/buildData'
import { REPO } from '../../lib/site'

const files = [
  { system: 'macOS with Apple silicon', file: 'PaperLab-<version>-arm64.dmg' },
  { system: 'macOS with an Intel processor', file: 'PaperLab-<version>-x64.dmg' },
  { system: 'Windows', file: 'PaperLab-<version>-x64.exe' },
  { system: 'Linux', file: 'PaperLab-<version>-x86_64.AppImage or PaperLab-<version>-amd64.deb' },
]
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Download PaperLab — desktop app for macOS, Windows, Linux"
  description="Install Docker Desktop, then download PaperLab from GitHub Releases for macOS, Windows or Linux. Free and open source. Or run it with two Docker commands."
>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <p class="eyebrow">Download</p>
      <h1>Install PaperLab in two steps</h1>
      <ol class="steps">
        <li>
          <h2>Install Docker Desktop and open it once</h2>
          <p class="muted">
            PaperLab runs its database and search in <a href="https://www.docker.com/products/docker-desktop/"
              >Docker</a
            >. OrbStack, Colima and Rancher Desktop work too. On Linux, install Docker from docker.com rather than the Snap
            package.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h2>Download PaperLab for your system and open it</h2>
          <p><DownloadButton where="download-page" label="Get the latest release" /></p>
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th scope="col">System</th><th scope="col">File</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              {
                files.map(({ system, file }) => (
                  <tr>
                    <td>{system}</td>
                    <td><code>{file}</code></td>
                  </tr>
                ))
              }
            </tbody>
          </table>
          <p class="muted">
            The first launch downloads PaperLab itself (about 335 MB) and opens it in its own window. A short setup offers
            a chat model and a search model. Both are optional: reading, highlighting and notes need neither.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>

      <h2>The first launch asks for permission</h2>
      <p class="muted">PaperLab isn't signed yet, so your system asks before the first launch and after each update.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>macOS:</strong> open PaperLab and close the warning, then go to System Settings → Privacy & Security and
          click <strong>Open Anyway</strong>.
        </li>
        <li><strong>Windows:</strong> on “Windows protected your PC”, click <strong>More info</strong>, then <strong>Run anyway</strong>.</li>
        <li>
          <strong>Linux:</strong> run <code>chmod +x PaperLab-*.AppImage</code> and start it. If it doesn't start, install
          the <code>.deb</code> instead.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Without the app</h2>
      <p class="muted">With Docker Compose 2.24 or later, two commands install the same release, with no clone and no build:</p>
      <pre><code>VERSION={latestVersion ?? '<version>'}   # the latest release's number
curl -L "https://github.com/khannoussi-malek/PaperLab/releases/download/v$VERSION/paperlab-$VERSION.tar.gz" | tar xz
cd paperlab && docker compose up -d   # then open http://127.0.0.1:5190</code></pre>
      <p class="muted">
        Updating, backups and building from source are in the <a href={`${REPO}#quick-start`}>README's Quick start</a>.
      </p>

      <aside class="card got-it">
        <h2>Got it running?</h2>
        <p class="muted">
          A star is the quickest way to help other researchers find PaperLab, and it puts each new release in your GitHub
          feed. If something broke on the way, <a href={`${REPO}/issues/new`}>open an issue</a>: install problems get fixed
          first.
        </p>
        <StarButton where="download-success" />
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .steps {
    padding-left: 1.25rem;
    margin: 2rem 0 3rem;
  }
  .steps li {
    margin-bottom: 2rem;
  }
  .steps h2 {
    font-size: 1.5rem;
  }
  h2 {
    font-size: 1.6rem;
    margin-top: 2rem;
  }
  table {
    width: 100%;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    margin: 1rem 0;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
  }
  th,
  td {
    text-align: left;
    padding: 0.6rem 0.5rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
    vertical-align: top;
  }
  td code {
    word-break: break-all;
  }
  ul li {
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
  }
  .got-it {
    margin-top: 3rem;
  }
  .got-it h2 {
    margin-top: 0;
  }
</style>
